The {{nihongo|'''Quiet Revolution'''|静かな公転|Shizukana Kōten}}, also known as the '''Red Revolution''', was a ten-month period of escalating civil unrest, mass protests, and political upheaval in [[Okaiken]] that began in October 1967 and culminated in the collapse of the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] on August 12, 1968.

The revolution began as a series of protests against the authoritarian-leaning government of [[Socialist Party (Okaiken)|Socialist]] Prime Minister [[Genzui Kusaka]], which had come to power in 1966 amid allegations of vote-rigging. The protests, which grew from student and labor movements, transformed into a "People Power"–style popular uprising following a second fraudulent election in June 1968.

This period of sustained civil resistance, which included a critical military defection, led to the ousting of Prime Minister Kusaka, the formation of the unified [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]], and the intervention of [[Emperor Wakayama]]. It directly resulted in the drafting of a new constitution and the proclamation of the Federal Republic of Okaiken on January 1, 1970.

==Background==

===Economic and Political Crisis===
The mid-1960s were a time of economic turmoil for Okaiken. The partition of neighboring [[Kwangju]] into three nations disrupted regional trade, and the [[Sanese En]] was hit by high inflation. This volatile combination led to the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] crashing on October 15, 1967, wiping out fortunes and enraging the public.

This economic anxiety was layered on top of a political crisis. The [[Okaiken general election, 1966|1966 general election]] was a "decisively close" victory for [[Genzui Kusaka]]'s [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Socialist Party]] over the [[Liberal Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Party]] led by [[Yoshimizu Ohno]]. The Liberals immediately cried foul, citing widespread "vote-rigging" and refusing to recognize the legitimacy of the Kusaka government. Led by Ohno, the Liberal Party and its allies boycotted the Diet, grinding the government to a halt.

===Kusaka's "Anti-Parliament" Bill===
Instead of seeking compromise, Prime Minister Kusaka's government responded with a series of authoritarian measures. In late 1967, the Socialists, using their slim, disputed majority, began drafting a bill to fundamentally restructure the Sanese state.

The proposed legislation included cutting government agencies, re-militarizing Okaiken, and, most controversially, a provision to abolish the elected parliament entirely. It was to be replaced with a "non-voting system where a select committee can select nominees from other parties."

This proposal was viewed by the public and the opposition as a blatant attempt to establish a one-party dictatorship. The bill's introduction was the primary trigger for the revolution.

==The Revolution==

===Phase 1: The Diet Blockade (Oct 1967 – May 1968)===
The "Anti-Parliament Bill" and the stock market crash on October 15, 1967, acted as dual catalysts. A massive, broad-based protest movement emerged, led by university students, labor unions (like the powerful ''Sanese General Workers' Union''), and opposition politicians.

For months, they blockaded the [[Diet of Okaiken]] building in [[Okami]]. Protesters, many wearing the red ribbons that gave the revolution its nickname, clashed repeatedly with the [[Okami Metropolitan Police Department|Okami Metropolitan Police]], who used tear gas and batons to clear the streets. The regime's "regime violence" against the peaceful student protesters was broadcast nightly on [[KTK]] and only served to increase public support for the opposition.

===Phase 2: The Stolen Election (June 1968)===
By the spring of 1968, the country was ungovernable. In an attempt to break the stalemate, [[Emperor Wakayama]] took the unusual step on May 12, 1968, of dissolving the deadlocked Diet and calling for a new snap election, scheduled for June 4.

The opposition parties, including Ohno's Liberals, agreed to participate, hoping to oust the Socialists at the ballot box. The election was held under a cloud of intimidation and military presence. On June 5, the results were announced: the Socialist Party had won again, by a "narrow victory" that the public immediately decried as fraudulent.

This "stolen election" was the revolution's final breaking point, where a fraudulent snap election served as the catalyst for a mass uprising.

===Phase 3: People Power (June 1968)===
Within hours of the election results, millions of Sanese civilians, wearing red ribbons, poured onto the streets of Okami, demanding Kusaka's immediate resignation. The protests of 1967 paled in comparison to the massive, non-violent uprising now underway.

The critical event occurred on June 6, 1968. General Ryohei Morita, the respected commander of the Okami Capital Defense Force, announced that he would not "fire upon the Sanese people" on behalf of a fraudulent government. He, along with his entire division, defected and fortified Camp Shiroyama, the main military base in the capital. Opposition leaders [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] and [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]] immediately went to the camp, appearing on the gates with General Morita in a public show of defiance.

Enraged, Prime Minister Kusaka declared the general and politicians traitors. He ordered loyalist OSDF units, including the [[Imperial Household Agency|Imperial Guard]], to advance on Camp Shiroyama and crush the "mutiny."

What happened next was the defining moment of the revolution. Responding to broadcasts from a pirate radio station, ''Radio Jiyū'' (Radio Freedom), hundreds of thousands of civilians—men, women, and children—flooded Sumida Avenue, the main boulevard to the camp. They formed a human barricade, blocking the path of the loyalist tanks and armored personnel carriers.

For three days, the standoff continued. Protesters offered food and red flowers to the loyalist soldiers, begging them not to shoot their own people. This "civilian shield," protecting the defecting soldiers, brought the nation to a standstill.

===Phase 4: The Emperor's Intervention and Collapse (August 1968)===
The stalemate was broken by the Emperor himself. On June 12, Emperor Wakayama, seeing that Kusaka was willing to plunge the nation into civil war, made an unprecedented live broadcast to the nation. He did not explicitly take sides, but instead invoked his "sincere desire for peace" and called for an end to all violence, stating that the "will of the people must be respected."

This broadcast, an incredibly powerful and rare "Sanese" solution, destroyed Kusaka's remaining authority. The loyalist tank commanders, unwilling to defy the Emperor, stopped their engines. Kusaka's government had lost the military and the people.

The political maneuvering was swift. The three other major opposition parties, seeing their unity, formally merged to create the '''[[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]]''' and elected [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] as their leader. This merger meant the opposition now held a clear, "legitimate" majority of seats from the (disputed) June 4 election.

On August 12, 1968, the Republican-controlled Diet formally unseated the Socialist Party, removing Kusaka from power.

==Aftermath==

The Quiet Revolution permanently broke the old political order and set Okaiken on a new, irreversible path. The immediate political fallout was the formal removal of [[Genzui Kusaka]] from power and the dissolution and banning of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] for its "authoritarian actions." This ban would last for twenty years, effectively removing the hard-line statist left from the political landscape.

[[Yoshimizu Ohno]], as the consensus leader of the newly-formed [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]], was installed as the head of a transitional "national unity" government on August 12, 1968. His primary mandate was to stabilize the nation and draft a new constitution.

===The "Imperial Question" and the Great Transformation===
The most complex issue facing the new government was the "Imperial Question." [[Emperor Wakayama]]'s broadcast on June 12 had been the decisive act that prevented civil war, earning him immense gratitude from the public. However, his intervention also exposed the profound, undemocratic power the Emperor still held under the Saito Constitution.

A great national debate began. Republicans, led by Ohno and a rising [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], argued that any supreme, unelected authority—even a benevolent one—was incompatible with a true republic. They pointed out that the Emperor's power to stop the tanks was the same power that could have ordered them to fire. Traditionalists and conservative hardliners were horrified, viewing any move against the Emperor as an ungrateful betrayal of the man who had saved the nation.

The crisis was resolved by Emperor Wakayama himself. In a series of private meetings with Ohno in late 1968, the Emperor "volunteered" to abdicate his political authority and support the transition to a federal republic. He stated that the Imperial role must evolve from a position of power to a "symbol of the people's unity and cultural heritage."

This decision, which stunned the nation, was a masterstroke. While it deeply saddened traditionalists, it completely defused the conflict, preventing a "second revolution" between the Republicans and Monarchists. This began the period known as the [[Great Transformation]], a peaceful, top-to-bottom restructuring of the Sanese state.

===The New Constitution and Federal Republic===
With the Emperor's blessing, Ohno's government drafted the 1969 Constitution. This new document, designed to prevent a future "Kusaka," was revolutionary:

It formally stripped the Emperor of all political power and "divine" status, redefining him in Article 1 as the "symbol of the State and the unity of the People."

It seized the vast, tax-exempt assets of the Imperial Household. Palaces, including the [[Shiroyama Teikoku Palace]], were turned into public parks, and their assets were used to help pay for the economic damage caused during the recession.

It enshrined new business regulations and expanded suffrage, firmly establishing the [[Diet of Okaiken]] as the sole source of power in the nation.

On January 1, 1970, the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] was formally dissolved, and the '''Federal Republic of Okaiken''' was declared. [[Yoshimizu Ohno]] served as its first Federal Prime Minister, overseeing the complex legal and social transition. The country's name was officially changed from "Mitoyagi" to "Okaiken" in July 1971 after a series of referendums.

The revolution also solidified a new political landscape. The [[Republican Party (Okaiken)|Republican Party]] became the dominant political force, effectively absorbing the old Liberal and Democratic parties. In 1973, Ohno stepped down, and his successor, [[Yasuhiro Nakasone]], led the RPO to a landslide victory in the new republic's first full elections, cementing the new order.

==Legacy==
The Quiet Revolution is considered the most important event in modern Okaiken history, in which a "People Power" movement, backed by a timely military defection and the intervention of the Emperor, successfully overthrew an authoritarian government without descending into a full-scale civil war.

